    "body": "@hsivonen @zetafunction @jdm @gterzian @avidrissman, if you have time to compare the current spec to what you see implemented, I'd love to partner on updating it.\n\nI think some of this has gotten better since our original posts here and in #546. In particular:\n\n- Which initial about:blank cases are done with \"replace\" vs. \"push\" history handling is centralized in [navigate](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#navigate) step 12 (deferring to [the navigation must be a replace](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#the-navigation-must-be-a-replace) in particular)\n- `iframe` insertion and `src=\"\"` change processing has gotten centralized in [process the `iframe` attributes](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#process-the-iframe-attributes)\n  - No-`src=\"\"`, empty-string `src=\"\"`, `src=\"about:blank\"`, and `src=\"about:blank?foo\"` are all processed the same way.\n  - There is a special case for inserting about:blank iframes to not do a full navigation, and to synchronously fire `load`. (See also the discussion in [completely finish loading](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#completely-finish-loading).)\n\nThe core remaining question for me is about `Window` object reuse. In https://github.com/web-platform-tests/wpt/pull/51895 @annevk has a test which seems to indicate the `Window` object is not reused in the way the spec indicates, at least in WebKit and Blink.\n\n`Window` object reuse is step 6 of https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#initialise-the-document-object . But surely it's more complicated than just removing that step, right? When do browsers reuse the `Window` object in reality?\n\nNote that `Window` object reuse is separate from history entry reuse. The latter is covered by the choice of \"replace\" vs. \"push\" history handling and seems pretty interoperable.",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
